tive map?", "role": "attempt", "is_correct": true, "has_media": false, "media": null}, {"timestamp": "2026-04-11T16:34:40Z", "username": "Srijan Naayak", "text": "Ahh", "role": "chat", "is_correct": null, "has_media": false, "media": null}, {"timestamp": "2026-04-11T16:34:47Z", "username": "Chinmay Mehta", "text": "It is called the universal corrective map", "role": "elaboration", "is_correct": null, "has_media": false, "media": null}, {"timestamp": "2026-04-11T16:35:01Z", "username": "Srijan Naayak", "text": "Ye to par question mein hi tha na ?", "role": "chat", "is_correct": null, "has_media": false, "media": null}, {"timestamp": "2026-04-11T16:35:41Z", "username": "Srijan Naayak", "text": "I thought ye to nahi hoga", "role": "chat", "is_correct": null, "has_media": false, "media": null}, {"timestamp": "2026-04-11T16:36:42Z", "username": "Srijan Naayak", "text": "Koi nahi chinmay bhai ab edit kardo late viewers ke liye", "role": "chat", "is_correct": null, "has_media": false, "media": null}, {"timestamp": "2026-04-11T16:37:14Z", "username": "Chinmay Mehta", "text": "Last q", "role": "elaboration", "is_correct": null, "has_media": false, "media": null}, {"timestamp": "2026-04-11T16:38:04Z", "username": "Chinmay Mehta", "text": "For most of recorded history, no European had any reliable knowledge of what lay at the North Pole. The Arctic Ocean was uncharted. But cartographers abhorred blank space — and their maps had edges.\nThe Flemish cartographer Gerardus Mercator — the same man whose 1569 projection would shape the Western spatial imagination for centuries — resolved this problem with characteristic confidence. In his 1595 atlas, he drew the North Pole not as open sea or honest blankness, but as a massive black magnetic rock, roughly the size of France, surrounded by four symmetrical islands and four rivers that channelled the world's ocean waters inward in an eternal vortex. He based the image partly on a medieval English travel narrative attributed to a friar named Nicholas of Lynn, and partly on speculation about why compass needles always pointed north.\nThe feature appeared faithfully on European maps for nearly a century. Sailors probing the Northwest Passage dutifully reported that no such rock had been encountered. But the image persisted, because it provided a satisfying answer to the question that preoccupied every navigator: why does the compass needle point north?\nWhat was Mercator's imaginary Arctic magnetic rock called, and what navigational mystery did its supposed existence purport to explain?", "role": "elaboration", "is_correct": null, "has_media": false, "media": null}, {"timestamp": "2026-04-11T16:38:39Z", "username": "Srijan Naayak", "text": "Magnetic Deviation ?", "role": "chat", "is_correct": null, "has_media": false, "media": null}, {"timestamp": "2026-04-11T16:38:56Z", "username": "మాन$", "text": "Atlas holding world", "role": "chat", "is_correct": null, "has_media": false, "media": null}, {"timestamp": "2026-04-11T16:39:21Z", "username": "Chinmay Mehta", "text": "Nota", "role": "elaboration", "is_correct": null, "has_media": false, "media": null}, {"timestamp": "2026-04-11T16:39:29Z", "username": "Pavan Pamidimarri", "text": "black rock?", "role": "chat", "is_correct": null, "has_media": false, "media": null}, {"timestamp": "2026-04-11T16:39:40Z", "username": "Chinmay Mehta", "text": "Yes, Latin name for it?", "role": "confirmation", "is_correct": null, "has_media": false, "media": null}]